<article class="env-lemma" id="069B">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/069B">Lemma <span data-tag="069B">37.61.10</span>.</a> The property $\mathcal{P}(f) =$“$f$ is perfect” is fpqc local on the base. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will use the criterion of Descent, Lemma <a href="/tag/02KP" data-tag="02KP">35.22.4</a> to prove this. By Definition <a href="/tag/0687" data-tag="0687">37.61.2</a> being perfect is Zariski local on the base. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0688" data-tag="0688">37.61.3</a> being perfect is preserved under flat base change. The final hypothesis (3) of Descent, Lemma <a href="/tag/02KP" data-tag="02KP">35.22.4</a> translates into the following algebra statement: Let $A \to B$ be a faithfully flat ring map. Let $C = A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/I$ be an $A$-algebra. If $C \otimes _ A B$ is perfect as an $B[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$-module, then $C$ is perfect as a $A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$-module. This is More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/068T" data-tag="068T">15.76.13</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>